#
# Test vectors for core.abnf, the ASPIS core rules (ADR 0062, rule 8;
# ADR 0013, rule 6).
#
# Each vector is one line: "ok", "err" or "errc", "@" and the rule it
# tests, a tab, then the octets as \xHH escapes (letters and digits stand
# for themselves). "errc" marks octets that match the rule's context-free
# grammar but break one of its constraints: tools/ci/check-grammars.py
# reads it as "ok", and crates/abnf, which also enforces the constraints,
# as "err". Lines starting with "#", and empty lines, are ignored.
#
# Directives: "# caps: N" sets the capability count, 0 to 3, for the lines
# that follow, and resets the unwrapped mask; "# unwrapped: M" sets the
# unwrapped mask, one hex digit. Both start at 0. Every line names its
# rule, so this file has no "# rule:" directive.

# ---- Integers: extremes, and one octet short or long ----
ok@u8	\x00
ok@u8	\xff
err@u8	
err@u8	\x00\x00
ok@u16	\x00\x00
ok@u16	\xff\xff
err@u16	\x00
err@u16	\x00\x00\x00
ok@u32	\x00\x00\x00\x00
ok@u32	\xff\xff\xff\xff
err@u32	\x00\x00\x00
err@u32	\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00
